The Nautilus DevOps team is conducting application deployment tests on selected application servers. 
They require a nginx container deployment on Application Server 3. 
Complete the task with the following instructions:

On Application Server 3 create a container named nginx_3 using the nginx image with the alpine tag. Ensure container is in a running state.

SOLUTION
To complete this task, you'll need to work on Application Server 3 (stapp03) in the Stratos Datacenter.

🔑 Server Credentials
App Server 3 (stapp03): User banner, Password BigGr33n

🛠️ Step-by-Step Implementation
1. Connect to Application Server 3
From the jump host, SSH into stapp03:

bash
ssh banner@stapp03
# Enter password: BigGr33n
2. Switch to root (or use sudo)
Docker commands typically require root privileges or membership in the docker group. Switch to root to avoid permission issues:

bash
sudo su -
3. Verify Docker is available
Check that Docker is installed and the daemon is running:

bash
docker --version
systemctl status docker
4. Pull the nginx:alpine image (optional but recommended)
Pulling the image first ensures you get a clean deployment and can catch any network issues early:

bash
docker pull nginx:alpine
5. Create and run the container
Run the container in detached mode (-d) with the name nginx_3, using the nginx:alpine image:

bash
docker run -d --name nginx_3 nginx:alpine
6. Verify the container is running
Confirm the container is up and in a running state:

bash
docker ps
You should see output similar to:

text
CONTAINER ID   IMAGE          COMMAND                  CREATED         STATUS         PORTS     NAMES
a1b2c3d4e5f6   nginx:alpine   "/docker-entrypoint.…"   5 seconds ago   Up 5 seconds   80/tcp    nginx_3
You can also verify with:

bash
docker ps -a --filter "name=nginx_3"
⚠️ Important Notes
Container name: Must be exactly nginx_3 — Docker container names are case-sensitive.

Image tag: Use nginx:alpine (not just nginx, which would pull latest).

Detached mode: The -d flag is required; without it, the container would run in the foreground and block your terminal.

No port mapping needed: The task only requires the container to be running, not accessible externally. If port mapping were needed, you would add -p <host_port>:80.

✅ Final Verification
Run this one-liner to confirm everything is correct:

bash
docker inspect --format='{{.Name}} - {{.State.Status}} - {{.Config.Image}}' nginx_3
Expected output:

text
/nginx_3 - running - nginx:alpine
Once you see the container in a running state, the task is complete.
